<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <!--
      A car in front of the sun, on a circular badge.

      The art is composed inside a disc of r=33 at (54,54) and everything outside that disc is
      painted back out at the end of this file. A launcher mask is not something the icon can
      rely on: it may be a circle, a squircle or a rounded square, so drawing the circle here is
      the only way the badge reads as a circle everywhere. It also settles the safe zone by
      construction. r=33 is exactly the 66dp safe zone, so art clipped to the badge cannot be
      clipped again by any mask, and the sun and the car can be cut off by the rim deliberately
      rather than by an OEM.

      The background layer behind this is RichBlack, so a non-circular mask shows a dark corner
      around the badge rather than a teal one, and the rim stays a real edge.

      Source art: sun and car outline icons from SVG Repo (svgrepo.com), kept in .design/ and
      converted here. Their 24x24 coordinates are preserved inside the groups below and placed by
      the group transforms, so the paths can still be diffed against the originals.
    -->

    <!-- The badge. -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#005F73"
        android:pathData="M21,54 a33,33 0 1,0 66,0 a33,33 0 1,0 -66,0 Z" />

    <!--
      The sun, behind. Gamboge is the warmest thing in the palette and the only entry that reads
      as yellow; it measures 3.23:1 on the badge, clearing the 3:1 a meaningful graphic needs.

      Stroke 1.5 in the source's own units, unchanged, which lands at 4.05 of the 108
      viewport. The rays are the one deviation: the source draws them from r=9 to r=10, a stub
      tuned for a 24px glyph, and at a third of the icon's width that renders as a dot because
      the stroke is wider than the ray is long. They run r=8.3 to r=11 here instead.
    -->
    <group
        android:scaleX="2.7"
        android:scaleY="2.7"
        android:translateX="11.60"
        android:translateY="8.60">
        <path
            android:fillColor="#00000000"
            android:strokeColor="#EE9B00"
            android:strokeWidth="1.5"
            android:pathData="M6,12 a6,6 0 1,0 12,0 a6,6 0 1,0 -12,0" />
        <path
            android:fillColor="#00000000"
            android:strokeColor="#EE9B00"
            android:strokeWidth="1.5"
            android:strokeLineCap="round"
            android:pathData="M20.3000 12.0000L23.0000 12.0000 M17.8690 17.8690L19.7782 19.7782 M12.0000 20.3000L12.0000 23.0000 M6.1310 17.8690L4.2218 19.7782 M3.7000 12.0000L1.0000 12.0000 M6.1310 6.1310L4.2218 4.2218 M12.0000 3.7000L12.0000 1.0000 M17.8690 6.1310L19.7782 4.2218" />
    </group>

    <!--
      The car, in front.

      The source is an outline with no fill, which would let the sun show straight through it and
      lose the depth the composition is for. So the silhouette is filled first and the source
      path is then stroked over it unaltered, which keeps the drawing's interior lines,
      the roof seam and the line the wheels hang from, instead of flattening it to a blob.

      Vanilla on Gamboge is 1.59:1, so a pale car laid over an amber sun would be one shape. The
      RichBlack line is what separates them, at 8.47:1 on the sun and 13.48:1 on the body, and
      the fill itself is 5.14:1 where the car overhangs onto the badge. A RichBlack silhouette
      instead of a pale one was the alternative and it fails the other way: 2.62:1 on the badge.

      Stroke 1.3 rather than the source's 1, which lands at 3.19 of the viewport and
      close to the sun's 4.05. The two source icons are drawn at different weights, 1 and
      1.5 of 24, and left alone they would not look like one drawing.
    -->
    <group
        android:scaleX="2.45"
        android:scaleY="2.45"
        android:translateX="35.600"
        android:translateY="35.825">
        <path
            android:fillColor="#E9D8A6"
            android:pathData="M6 10L6.72147 5.6712C6.8822 4.70683 7.71658 4 8.69425 4H15.3057C16.2834 4 17.1178 4.70683 17.2785 5.6712L18 10C19.1046 10 20 10.8954 20 12V14C20 15.1046 19.1046 16 18 16L17.9999 17.5001C17.9999 18.3284 17.3283 19 16.4999 19C15.6715 19 15 18.3284 15 17.5001V16H8.9999V17.5001C8.9999 18.3284 8.32835 19 7.49995 19C6.67155 19 6 18.3284 6 17.5001V16C4.89543 16 4 15.1046 4 14V12C4 10.8954 4.89543 10 6 10Z" />
        <path
            android:fillColor="#00000000"
            android:strokeColor="#001219"
            android:strokeWidth="1.3"
            android:strokeLineCap="round"
            android:strokeLineJoin="round"
            android:pathData="M6 10L6.72147 5.6712C6.8822 4.70683 7.71658 4 8.69425 4H15.3057C16.2834 4 17.1178 4.70683 17.2785 5.6712L18 10M6 10H18M6 10C4.89543 10 4 10.8954 4 12V14C4 15.1046 4.89543 16 6 16M18 10C19.1046 10 20 10.8954 20 12V14C20 15.1046 19.1046 16 18 16M18 16H6M18 16L17.9999 17.5001C17.9999 18.3284 17.3283 19 16.4999 19C15.6715 19 15 18.3284 15 17.5001V16H18ZM6 16V17.5001C6 18.3284 6.67155 19 7.49995 19C8.32835 19 8.9999 18.3284 8.9999 17.5001V16H6Z" />
    </group>

    <!--
      The rim. A viewport-sized rectangle with the badge punched out of it, filled with the
      background colour, so every stroke above is cut off exactly at the circle. Even-odd rather
      than a clip-path: a fill is antialiased the same way the rest of the art is, where
      VectorDrawable's clip-path is a hard-edged canvas clip.
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#001219"
        android:fillType="evenOdd"
        android:pathData="M0,0 H108 V108 H0 Z M21,54 a33,33 0 1,0 66,0 a33,33 0 1,0 -66,0 Z" />
</vector>
